Hello team,

I have a few additional questions:

* Since when has the debt been accumulating? Was the debt formed under all submitted agreements?
* If earlier debts were not paid, why were new agreements concluded?


According to the 2024 agreement, which, as I understand, is still in force, the rules for issuing and submitting invoices are set out in Section 3. 

The main provisions in short:

* Payments are made for services actually provided properly, on time, and with good quality, and when services are provided in stages – after completion of the respective stage;
* The Supplier undertakes to submit an electronic VAT invoice to the Client no later than by the 4th (fourth) working day of the following month. The Parties agree that the fact of service delivery will be evidenced by the electronic VAT invoice detailing the services provided;
* The Supplier must ensure that electronic invoices conforming to the European electronic invoicing standard are submitted using means of the Supplier’s choice;
* Electronic invoices not meeting the European Standard on Electronic Invoicing may only be submitted via the “SABIS” information system;
* The Client pays for the duly provided services no later than within 30 (thirty) calendar days from the date the electronic VAT invoice is received and approved in the “SABIS” system, in accordance with the procedure set out in the Contract;
* If the Supplier fails to comply with the invoicing deadlines and procedure set out in this Agreement and/or in legislation, payments may be withheld. Such withholding of payments is not considered a breach of the Agreement terms (i.e., no late payment penalties accrue).

Taking the above into account, I provide answers to the questions raised:

* The Client must pay for services that are properly and timely delivered;
* The Supplier may choose how invoices are submitted if they meet the specified standard;
* If invoices do not meet the standard, they must be submitted through the SABIS system;
* If the Supplier fails to comply with the invoicing rules, the Client may withhold payments;
* Nowhere in the Agreement is it stated that if the Supplier delays invoice submission, the Client may refuse to pay for services already rendered.

In this case, I understand the dispute arises only because the Supplier failed to submit invoices properly and on time. In such a case, I would recommend negotiating with the university on proper invoice submission and signing a debt reconciliation statement (or similar), on the basis of which the university would reimburse the outstanding amounts. 


At this stage, for accounting matters, I think [~accountid:712020:2b04881a-4ca8-40cb-9a35-a008b9853ec0] could consult us best! 

Thanks!

