Hey, [~accountid:712020:72f2a8d2-216e-40e7-bfa9-9284c7d2e514] - thanks for that. On the below:

{quote}Employees will register directly with SPHD via their membership application form. SPHD is responsible for collecting and managing all necessary employee consents for data use and credit assessment during that process. Bolt will not be involved in the sign-up or ongoing administration of the program.{quote}

Will SPHD want to verify the employment relationship of such an individual? If not, correct me if I am wrong please, but it seems that we will not be sharing any personal data of our employees with them - is that correct?

Hey CSPC,

As mentioned below, we have discontinued this approach for two main reasons. First, under data protection law we are not obliged to generate new data sets in response to a DSR. The EDPB Guidelines 01/2022 on the right of access clarify that controllers must provide the personal data undergoing processing, but not create new documents, analyses, or compilations that are not already maintained in that form. Second, fulfilling such requests would be highly resource-intensive, as it would require to download, re-order, and redact the data (names, ratings, and any other identifiers). Doing this once would create an expectation that we honour similar requests going forward, which is not sustainable.

While we understand the driver’s situation, exceptions cannot be made.

Please revert to the driver with the following message:

{quote}Dear [name],

Thank you for your message. 

After a diligent review, we confirm that we are unable to provide the information in the manner requested. In line with applicable data protection regulations, your right of access entitles you to obtain confirmation as to whether personal data concerning you are being processed and to receive a copy of such personal data. This right, however, does not oblige Bolt to generate new representations of personal or service-related data, documents, restructure existing data sets, or create compilations of information that are not maintained in that form in our systems. In addition, your right of access must be balanced against the rights and freedoms of other individuals, which may restrict the extent to which certain information can be disclosed. Accordingly, we are unable to provide the comments you requested, as this would require creating a new data set and may risk revealing personal data of other individuals. We apologise for any inconvenience this may cause.

For information on how we process relevant personal data, we refer you to our Privacy Policy at [https://bolt.eu/en/privacy/|https://bolt.eu/en/privacy/|smart-link] , which includes further information about:

* The categories of personal data we process;
* The purposes for which we process personal data;
* The recipients or categories of recipients to whom the personal data has been or will be disclosed;
* The criteria we use to determine how long to retain personal data;
* The sources of personal data received from other parties;
* Your rights in relation to your personal data;
* The existence and envisaged consequences of any automated decision-making/profiling of personal data; and
* Details of any transfer of your personal data to third countries or international organisations and the safeguards used for any such transfers.

*Your rights* 

 In addition to your right to access any personal data we hold about you, you have the right to request us to rectify, erase your personal data, restrict the processing of your personal data or to object to such processing. Any such requests that are received will be considered in conjunction with the applicable provisions of relevant data protection laws. 

If you are dissatisfied with this response to your request or in relation to any other matter relating to the processing of your personal data, we hope you will continue to work with us to resolve them. You also have the right to complain directly to your data protection/supervisory authority and, depending on the applicable data protection laws in your country, to seek a judicial remedy.

If you have any additional questions, please contact our Customer Support Team by replying to this email.

Yours sincerely,

Bolt{quote}

Thanks [~accountid:712020:a25cc8d9-692b-4200-aebf-19e86c575441] - I have taken a look at this and my thoughts are as follows:

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Question*
{panel}

Is Bolt transferring any personal data to the Agency? Or is Bolt only the *_recipient_* of personal data here (i.e., the data of the Agency’s representatives)? 

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Advice*
{panel}

If Bolt is only the recipient of personal data under this arrangement, then the {color:#36b37e}*agreement looks fine from a privacy POV*{color} ✅ 

I would {color:#4c9aff}*recommend including the following amendments*{color} for good measure to protect Bolt’s position. However, these are not deal-breakers if it would be operationally unfeasible to include then:

* *Clause 1.3.1:* Amend to say “_…otherwise invasive of privacy_ +_(in particular, capturing identifiable third-party individuals without first obtaining lawful permissions)_+” 
* *Clause 11.1:* Add the following at the end of the provision: “_The Agency warrants that: (i) it has provided privacy notices that comply with applicable data protection laws_ t_o relevant data subjects; and (ii) it has a valid legal basis to collect and disclose the personal data of these data subject to Bolt for the purposes of this Agreement_.”

Is anything further needed from the Privacy Team on this? Let me know if not, in which case I will close the ticket 🙂

